      "shortName": "Big Bear",
      "region": "SoCal",
      "lat": 34.2275,
      "lon": -116.8605,
      "baseFt": 7140,
      "summitFt": 8805,
      "summit": { "lat": 34.208, "lon": -116.8345 },
      "tz": "America/Los_Angeles",
      "local": {
        "notes": [
          "Bear Mountain runs 7,140-8,805 ft and Snow Summit 7,000-8,200 ft. Each averages only about 100 inches of natural snow a season (season totals have ranged from 39 to 243 inches), so the resort relies on extensive snowmaking.",
          "Snowmaking starts when overnight temperatures dip into the low 20s with low humidity and light wind, usually early to mid November; crews want 48-72 hours of those conditions, and dry Santa Ana air helps.",
          "Warm storms, including atmospheric rivers, often bring high snow levels and rain on the mountain or only a few inches. Cold storms from the north can produce a foot or more even with limited moisture.",
          "Openings from 2015/16 to 2023/24 fell between November 12 and December 8."
        ],
        "sources": [
